@* Exercise 074 - Virtualize Fixed Item Size (advanced).
   Goal:   Control how big the realised window is, and show something in the gap
           while a slow page is still loading.
   Drills: <Virtualize>'s ItemSize and OverscanCount, and its Placeholder fragment,
           which is what fills the window until the provider answers.
   Passes: dotnet test --filter FullyQualifiedName~Ex074_ *@

@* TODO: render a <Virtualize> over Provider with BOTH fragments, passing ItemSize
   and OverscanCount straight through:
     ItemContent   <div class="row">…</div> with the item's text.
     Placeholder   <div class="placeholder">…</div> - what a slot shows when its
                   item is not there yet, which happens whenever the provider
                   answers with fewer items than the request asked for.
   The two knobs do different jobs, and the tests pin down both: OverscanCount
   widens the realised window, while ItemSize never changes how many rows exist -
   it sizes the spacer <div> that stands in for all the rows that do not. *@

@code {
    [Parameter] public Func<ItemsProviderRequest, ValueTask<ItemsProviderResult<string>>> Provider { get; set; }
        = _ => ValueTask.FromResult(new ItemsProviderResult<string>([], 0));

    [Parameter] public float ItemSize { get; set; } = 20f;
    [Parameter] public int OverscanCount { get; set; } = 3;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
        => throw new NotImplementedException("TODO: Ex074 - render the sized list with a placeholder");
}
